The code statements that lead to the bug are:
for (i = crc8->from_idx; i <= crc8->to_idx; i++)
for (i = crc8->from_idx; i >= crc8->to_idx; i--)
cf->data[crc8->result_idx] = crc ^ crc8->final_xor_val;
Reason:
The loop and the result write use the raw `s8` fields (`crc8->from_idx`, `crc8->to_idx`, `crc8->result_idx`) which can be negative, causing out-of-bounds heap access. The bounds check using `calc_idx()` passes because it returns a safe non‑negative index, but the actual access still uses the raw values.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
for (i = from; i <= to; i++)
for (i = from; i >= to; i--)
cf->data[res] = crc ^ crc8->final_xor_val;
Reason:
Replacing the raw `crc8->*_idx` with the computed safe variables (`from`, `to`, `res`) prevents negative index access, ensuring all operations stay within the allocated buffer.

